// Concept texts, batch 8 (names NEOLOGIST to POSSESSIVENESS).
// Row: [name, group code, short, feels, looks, misreading, help]. An empty help text uses the group's default.
// Group codes: P phobia, A anxiety, M mood, D disorder, T thinking pattern, C control or manipulation tactic,
// R trait or relationship pattern, V harmful habit, U strength or virtue, K practice or tool,
// F fascination or urge, B body or medical.
export default [
  ["NEOLOGIST", "T", "Someone who invents new words or uses made-up terms in everyday speech.", "Making up words can feel creative and playful, and it can bring a sense of freedom. Some people do this when ordinary words do not fit what they feel.", "Others may notice invented words, or a speaker whose meaning is hard to follow.", "Inventing words is common in art, play and science. It can be a concern when the words confuse others and distress the speaker.", ""],
  ["NEPIOPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in infants or very young children, which is serious and harmful when acted on.", "The interest can bring shame, fear and a sense of being trapped. Having these thoughts does not mean someone has acted on them, and early help can prevent harm.", "Often nothing is visible, because the interest is usually kept private. Secret or unsupervised time with children is a warning sign to take seriously.", "A feeling is not the same as acting on it, and help can start before anyone is harmed. Acting on sexual interest in children always harms them and is a serious crime.", "Sexual interest in children is serious, and acting on it harms children. Stop It Now offers a free, confidential helpline for people worried about their own thoughts or behavior, or someone else's: 1-888-773-8368. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["NERVOUS SYSTEM COHERENCE", "U", "A steady balance between the body's alert and calm states, so stress can rise and settle.", "Coherence can feel settled and grounded, with fewer sudden jolts of tension. It often shows up as a sense that the body can handle what comes.", "Others may notice a calm, steady presence, with breathing that stays relaxed under pressure.", "Coherence is a general way to describe calm, not a fixed trait or a test result. It can be built with practice and rest.", ""],
  ["NERVOUS SYSTEM DYSREGULATION", "B", "A state in which the body's alarm and calm systems struggle to return to balance after stress.", "The body may stay on high alert, or it may shut down and feel flat. Small things can trigger strong feelings, and recovery can take much longer than expected.", "Others may notice sudden anger, tears, restlessness, or a person who seems far away.", "This is not a character flaw or a lack of effort. It is a body response that can be understood and gently retrained over time.", ""],
  ["NERVOUS SYSTEM REPATTERNING", "K", "A practice of slowly retraining how the body responds to stress, with safe and repeated steps.", "Repatterning can feel slow and uneven at first. Over time, the body may learn that stress can rise and then pass.", "Others may notice a calmer tone and more flexible responses to pressure.", "It is not a quick fix or a single exercise. It usually works best with a trained therapist and many small, repeated steps.", ""],
  ["NERVOUS SYSTEM RESET", "K", "A short practice, such as slow breathing or a brief pause, to calm the body after stress.", "A reset can bring a noticeable drop in tension, like shoulders softening or the breath slowing. The effect often fades, so repeating it helps.", "Others may notice a pause before replying, a slower voice, or a person stepping outside for air.", "A reset is not a cure for panic, trauma or depression. It is a small tool that can make the next hour easier to handle.", ""],
  ["NEUROFIBROMATOSIS", "B", "An inherited condition that causes growths on nerves and often changes in the skin.", "Growths and skin marks can be worrying, and they may change how a person sees their body. Some people feel tired or anxious about future checkups.", "Others may notice spots on the skin, small lumps under the skin, or differences in learning or movement.", "Many growths from this condition are harmless, but some need checks. Each person's pattern can be very different.", ""],
  ["NEUROPLASTIC EMPOWERMENT", "U", "A hopeful sense that the brain can change through practice, effort and new experiences.", "This idea can bring hope, especially after long stress or setbacks. It can make new habits feel more possible.", "Others may notice a person who keeps trying new skills and talks about growth.", "Brain change takes time and repeated practice. Positive thinking alone does not rewire the brain, and it is not a guarantee of quick results.", ""],
  ["NIGHTMARE DISORDER", "D", "A sleep condition with frequent, distressing nightmares that wake a person and disrupt sleep.", "Waking from a bad dream can leave a person shaken, with a racing heart. Dreading sleep itself is common, and it can make rest feel unsafe.", "Others may notice restless sleep, cries in the night, or a person who seems tired.", "Nightmares are not a sign of weakness or a warning about the future. They are a sleep problem that often responds to treatment and planning.", ""],
  ["NIGHTMARE INTEGRATION", "K", "A practice of writing about nightmares and noticing their feelings, to lessen their hold.", "Looking at a nightmare can feel uncomfortable at first. Over time, it may bring a sense of control and a little less fear at bedtime.", "Others may notice a person keeping a dream journal or talking about dreams more calmly.", "This practice does not mean the nightmare was wanted or deserved. It is a way to understand distress, and it works best with support when nightmares are frequent.", ""],
  ["NIGHTMAREPHILE", "F", "A fascination with nightmares, frightening dreams, or the eerie feelings they bring.", "Scary dreams can feel thrilling or strangely meaningful. Some people enjoy the shiver of dread, and others feel a pull to revisit it.", "Others may notice a taste for horror stories, dream journals about frightening scenes, or talk about nightmares often.", "Enjoying a scary story is common and healthy for many people. It becomes a concern when fear keeps you from sleep or upsets your daily life.", ""],
  ["NIGHTMARES", "A", "Frightening dreams that can wake a person with fear, a racing heart or a sense of danger.", "Waking up can bring a rush of fear that takes time to fade. The dream may return in the mind during the day, which can make the next night harder.", "Others may notice shouting or thrashing in sleep, or a person who seems shaken after waking.", "Nightmares are common, especially during stress, illness or after upsetting events. Frequent nightmares are worth mentioning to a doctor, but they are not a character flaw.", ""],
  ["NIHILISTIC", "T", "A belief that life has no real meaning, so nothing seems worth caring about.", "The view can feel honest and heavy, as if hope is naive. It can also bring numbness, because effort seems pointless.", "Others may notice dismissive remarks such as 'nothing matters,' a flat tone, or little interest in plans.", "A belief that nothing matters is a thought, not a proven fact. Feeling that way is often linked to stress, low mood or exhaustion, and those can be treated.", ""],
  ["NIHILOPHILE", "F", "A fascination with nihilism, the idea that nothing has meaning, and with arguments for it.", "Dark ideas about meaning can feel exciting, clever or freeing for a while. Some people feel drawn to the bleak honesty of the view.", "Others may notice philosophical debates about meaning, dark jokes, or a focus on how pointless things are.", "Studying nihilism is not the same as despair. Many people explore these questions and still build a meaningful life.", ""],
  ["NOMOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of being without a phone, or of losing signal, battery or access to it.", "Being apart from a phone can bring anxiety, a racing heart or a sense of isolation. The fear can grow when the phone is out of reach.", "Others may notice constant phone checks or distress when the phone is missing.", "It is not just a joke or a sign of weakness, because the fear is real. It often eases with small, steady steps away from the screen.", ""],
  ["NON-24-HOUR", "B", "A body clock that runs longer than a day, so sleep and waking shift later each day.", "Sleep can feel out of step with daily life, and tiredness may come at odd hours. Many people feel frustrated, isolated or worried about work and relationships.", "Others may notice a person who is often late, sleeps at unusual hours, or seems exhausted at social events.", "This is not laziness or poor sleep habits. It is a timing problem in the body clock, and it can often be managed.", ""],
  ["NON-APOLOGY", "C", "An apology that avoids owning the harm, often shifting blame onto the person who was hurt.", "The person who was hurt may feel dismissed, confused or angry. The conflict often stays unresolved, and they may start to doubt whether their feelings count.", "Others may hear lines like 'I am sorry you feel that way' with no admission of harm.", "A real apology names the harm and takes responsibility. A non-apology is not proof that the other person is too sensitive.", ""],
  ["NON-SUICIDAL SELF-INJURY", "V", "Deliberately hurting your own body, such as cutting or burning, without meaning to die.", "Hurting the body can bring short relief from overwhelming feelings, followed by shame or fear. The urge may return quickly, which can feel frightening and hard to control.", "Others may notice unexplained cuts or burns, long sleeves in warm weather, or frequent injuries with vague explanations.", "This is not attention-seeking or a character flaw. It signals deep pain that deserves care, and it can ease with support.", "If you are hurting yourself or thinking about it, call or text 988 in the US now. Also call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["NOSOPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of catching a disease or becoming seriously ill.", "Thoughts of illness can bring dread, a racing heart and repeated body checks. Relief rarely lasts, and the fear often returns.", "Others may notice frequent health searches, avoidance of crowds or hospitals, or constant questions about symptoms.", "Fear of illness is not the same as being sick. Many people feel this way at times, and treatment for anxiety can make it easier to live with.", ""],
  ["NOT ENOUGH", "M", "A deep feeling that you are never good enough, no matter what you do or achieve.", "The feeling can bring constant pressure to prove yourself, and praise may not seem to land. Rest can feel undeserved or unsafe.", "Others may notice overwork, frequent apologies, or quick dismissal of your own successes.", "This feeling is a belief, not a measure of your worth. Many people carry it, and it often softens with support and time.", ""],
  ["NOURISHING SELF-CARE", "K", "A practice of caring for your body, mind and needs in ways that restore your energy.", "Caring for yourself can feel warm and steadying, even in small ways. It can also feel awkward at first if you are used to putting others first.", "Others may notice regular breaks, simple meals, and a clear no when rest is needed.", "Self-care is not indulgence or selfishness. It helps people keep going, but it does not replace needed treatment.", ""],
  ["NUMBED", "M", "Feeling emotionally flat, as if you cannot reach joy, sadness or love.", "The mind may feel muffled or far away, and feelings seem dim or out of reach. This can be frightening, and it may bring guilt about not feeling more.", "Others may notice a flat tone, little reaction to good or bad news, or withdrawal from people and plans.", "Numbness is a protective response to stress, not proof that you do not care. It often shifts as stress eases and support grows.", ""],
  ["NUMBNESS", "B", "A loss of feeling in part of the body, which can be physical, emotional or both.", "Numb skin can feel strange, heavy or like it belongs to someone else. Tingling, pins and needles, or weakness may come with it.", "Others may notice dropped objects, clumsy movement, or a person who does not react to heat, cold or touch.", "Numbness is often harmless and temporary, but it is not always so. Sudden numbness on one side, or with trouble speaking, is an emergency.", ""],
  ["NURTURING INNER CHILD", "K", "A practice of caring for the younger part of yourself with warmth, patience and comfort.", "Speaking warmly to an old, hurt part of you can bring tenderness and tears. Some people feel silly at first, and that is normal.", "Others may notice a person speaking kindly to themselves, or pausing to comfort a strong feeling.", "This is not childish or a way of staying stuck in the past. It is a way to meet old pain with care, so the present feels safer.", ""],
  ["NYCTOPHILIA", "F", "A love of the night, its quiet, darkness and sense of space.", "Night can feel calm, private and full of possibility. Some people feel most like themselves after dark.", "Others may notice a preference for evening walks, late quiet hours, or stargazing.", "Liking the night is a healthy preference, not a sign of low mood or danger. It becomes a concern only if it disrupts sleep or daily life.", ""],
  ["NYCTOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of the night or darkness, often with dread of being alone in the dark.", "Darkness can bring a rush of fear, a racing heart and a sense that danger is near. The fear may ease with light, company or routine.", "Others may notice a need for a light on at night, reluctance to be alone after dark, or avoidance of night outings.", "The fear is real, even when nothing dangerous is present. It often eases with gradual steps and support.", ""],
  ["NYMPHOMANIAC", "F", "An outdated, stigmatizing term for a very strong or frequent sexual drive.", "A strong sexual drive can feel intense or hard to manage, and it can bring shame or confusion. The label itself can make that shame worse.", "Others may notice a strong focus on sex that strains work, friendships or a relationship.", "A high sex drive is not a disease or a moral failing. Desire varies widely, and the term should not be used to judge anyone.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["OBLIGATION CREATION", "C", "A tactic of making someone feel they owe you, so they comply out of guilt or duty.", "The target can feel guilty, trapped and pressured to repay favors that were never agreed to. Saying no may bring fear of anger or withdrawal.", "Others may notice reminders of past help, sighs about sacrifices, or pressure to repay favors on demand.", "Helping someone is not a debt to be repaid on demand. Healthy give and take is fair, but pressure that never ends is a form of control.", ""],
  ["OBLIVIOPHILE", "F", "A rarely used term that may mean a fascination with oblivion, or with being forgotten.", "Thoughts of fading away can feel calming for a moment, or heavy and tempting. If they turn into a wish to stop existing, that is a sign to reach out now.", "Others may notice talk about wanting to disappear, be forgotten, or stop existing, often with little interest in the future.", "A passing thought about being forgotten is not the same as a plan. Any wish to stop existing deserves serious, caring attention.", "If you are thinking about ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now. Also call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["OBSESSION", "T", "A persistent, unwanted thought or image that keeps returning and feels urgent.", "The thought can feel sticky and impossible to shake, and it can bring fear, shame or disgust. Trying to push it away often makes it louder.", "Others may notice a person who keeps returning to one topic, asks the same question, or seems preoccupied.", "Having a strong interest in something is not an obsession. Obsessive thoughts are unwanted and distressing, and they can be treated.", ""],
  ["OBSESSIVE", "R", "Describes someone whose focus on a topic, order or detail goes beyond what is useful.", "Tight focus can bring a sense of control, but it can also feel exhausting. Letting go of a detail may bring real discomfort.", "Others may notice repeated checking, rigid routines, or conversations that keep returning to one subject.", "Being thorough is not the same as being obsessive. The pattern becomes a problem when it takes over time, rest or relationships.", ""],
  ["OBSESSIVE-COMPULSIVE PERSONALITY DISORDER", "D", "A long-running pattern of rigid rules, perfectionism and control that can strain work and relationships.", "Order and rules can feel like safety, and change can feel threatening. Mistakes may bring strong shame or anxiety.", "Others may notice inflexible routines, difficulty delegating tasks, or a strong focus on details that slows progress.", "This is not the same as obsessive-compulsive disorder, which involves unwanted thoughts and urges. This pattern is treatable, and therapy can help people loosen rigid habits.", ""],
  ["OCHLOMANIAC", "F", "A strong fascination with crowds and large gatherings.", "Busy places can feel energizing, full of voices and movement. For some people the pull toward crowds can feel hard to resist.", "Others may notice a preference for festivals, busy markets or packed events, and restlessness when alone.", "Enjoying a crowd is common and healthy. It becomes a concern only when it leads to risk, or when being alone feels unbearable.", ""],
  ["OMBROPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of rain, or of being caught out in wet weather.", "Rain can bring a rush of worry, tension and an urge to get indoors fast. The fear can grow with each storm forecast.", "Others may notice avoidance of outdoor plans, checking the weather often, or leaving early when clouds gather.", "The fear is real, even though rain is harmless for most people. It often responds well to gradual, supported exposure.", ""],
  ["OMORASHI", "F", "A sexual interest linked to the feeling of urgently needing the toilet.", "This interest can bring shame or confusion, especially if it feels hard to explain. It is not a choice made to hurt anyone.", "Usually private, shown in fantasies or private searches.", "Having an interest does not mean acting on it. Any sexual act without clear, ongoing consent is harm.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["ONE-UPPER", "R", "A person who needs to top every story, experience or hardship someone else shares.", "Others may feel brushed aside, as if their experience does not count. The one-upper may feel a need to stay ahead to feel secure.", "Others may notice quick comparisons, interrupted stories, and replies that always go bigger.", "Sharing a similar experience is caring. The pattern becomes harmful when every story is turned into a contest.", ""],
  ["ONIOMANIAC", "V", "An urge to buy things compulsively, often for relief or excitement that does not last.", "Buying can bring a quick lift, then regret or stress over money and clutter. The urge can return quickly, and it can be hard to resist.", "Others may notice unopened packages, frequent online orders, or money worries that seem to recur.", "Enjoying shopping is normal. The habit becomes harmful when it repeats despite regret or causes strain at home.", ""],
  ["ONOMATOMANIAC", "T", "An unusual fixation on a particular word or name, with a need to repeat or think about it.", "The word can keep looping in the mind, and it may feel important or hard to let go. Saying it aloud can bring a brief sense of relief.", "Others may notice repeated use of one word, writing it many times, or talk that keeps circling one name.", "A word stuck in the mind is common when people are tired or stressed. It is worth noting if it keeps interfering with daily life.", ""],
  ["OPENMINDED", "U", "Willing to consider new ideas, and to change your view when the evidence calls for it.", "Openness can feel curious and relaxed. It can also feel vulnerable, because changing a view takes humility.", "Others may notice a person asking questions, listening fully and saying 'I had not thought of that.'", "Being open-minded does not mean agreeing with everything. You can weigh an idea carefully and still keep your own position.", ""],
  ["OPHIDIOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of snakes, even harmless ones, including pictures or sounds of them.", "A snake, or even an image of one, can bring a jolt of fear, a racing heart and an urge to step back. The reaction can feel out of proportion, but it is real.", "Others may notice avoidance of hiking trails or gardens, freezing at the sight of a snake, or leaving a room.", "The fear is not a sign of weakness, and it is common. Gradual exposure, with support, helps many people feel calmer around snakes.", ""],
  ["OPIOID USE DISORDER", "D", "A medical condition in which opioid drug use causes harm and a loss of control.", "Cravings can feel urgent, and stopping can bring withdrawal, the body's reaction to going without the drug. Shame often keeps people from asking for help.", "Others may notice missed work, money or medicine going missing, changes in sleep, or pulling away from people.", "This is a medical condition, not a lack of willpower or a character flaw. Treatment with medicine and counseling works well for many people.", ""],
  ["OPPOSITIONAL", "R", "A tendency to resist, argue against or push back on requests and expectations.", "Pushing back can feel like protecting independence, though it can also bring conflict and isolation. Some people feel they must resist to avoid feeling controlled.", "Others may notice contrary answers, refusing simple requests, or arguing even about small matters.", "Questioning rules is not always a problem. The pattern matters when resistance is automatic and damages relationships or goals.", ""],
  ["OPPOSITIONAL DEFIANCE", "R", "Open refusal to follow rules or requests, often mixed with anger toward those in charge.", "Defiance can feel like standing up for yourself, but it can also come with hot anger and regret. It may feel safer than admitting a need for help.", "Others may notice slammed doors, refusal to comply with reasonable requests, or deliberately annoying people in authority.", "Defiance is often a response to feeling powerless, hurt or misunderstood. It can change when the person feels heard and has clear, fair limits.", ""],
  ["OPPOSITIONAL DEFIANT DISORDER", "D", "A childhood condition with a long pattern of anger, arguing and defiance toward adults.", "A child with this pattern may feel angry, misunderstood or overwhelmed. Family life and school can become very tense.", "Others may notice frequent arguments with adults, temper outbursts, or deliberate refusal to follow simple rules.", "It is not only a parenting problem, and the child is not choosing to be bad. Early, specialized support can change the path.", ""],
  ["OPTIMISTIC", "U", "Expecting good outcomes, and seeing setbacks as temporary rather than as proof of failure.", "Hope can feel light and steadying, and it can make hard days easier to face. Optimism can also feel fragile when things go wrong.", "Others may notice a person who looks for options, speaks with hope about the future, and bounces back faster.", "Optimism is not denial or forced cheerfulness. It means expecting that effort can make a difference, while still seeing real problems.", ""],
  ["ORIENTING CAPACITY", "K", "The ability to turn toward your surroundings and sense whether you are safe in the present moment.", "Looking around the room and noticing familiar objects can bring a sense of grounding. Some people find that this steadies a body that feels on alert.", "Others may notice a person slowly looking around, turning the head, or naming what they see before settling.", "It is not about ignoring fear or pretending danger is absent. It checks what is real right now, which can soften a body stuck on threat.", ""],
  ["ORIGINAL", "U", "Able to think and create in fresh, independent ways that feel true to you.", "Original thinking can feel exciting and a little lonely at times. It can bring a sense of pride when your own view takes shape.", "Others may notice fresh ideas, unusual solutions, or a person who does not simply follow the crowd.", "Being original does not mean being odd for the sake of it. It means thinking for yourself and still listening to others.", ""],
  ["ORNITHOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of birds, including the sudden flap of wings or a bird's close approach.", "A bird nearby can bring a jolt of fear, a racing heart and a strong urge to move away. The fear can feel silly afterwards, though it was real.", "Others may notice avoidance of parks, stepping back from birds, or flinching when wings move near them.", "The fear is real, even if birds are harmless to most people. Gradual, supported exposure can help people feel calmer around them.", ""],
  ["OTHELLO SYNDROME", "D", "A fixed, false belief that a partner is being unfaithful, despite no real evidence.", "The suspicion can feel completely certain and frightening, and it can drive constant checking. Doubt about the partner can feel impossible to settle.", "Others may notice repeated questions about whereabouts, checking phones, or accusations with no proof.", "A feeling of certainty is not evidence. This is a real condition that responds to treatment, and it can hurt both people in a relationship.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED ADVERSE EFFECT OF MEDICATION", "B", "A label for a medicine side effect that causes distress but fits no named category.", "Side effects can feel frightening, uncomfortable or confusing, especially when no one can name them. The body may seem to change without warning.", "Others may notice new tiredness, nausea, mood shifts or other changes after a new medicine starts.", "A side effect is real even when it has no standard name. Never stop a prescribed medicine suddenly without advice from the doctor who prescribed it.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED DEPRESSIVE DISORDER", "D", "A label for depression-like symptoms that are real and distressing but fit no standard category.", "Low mood, heaviness or lost interest can feel constant and wearing. The lack of a standard name can make the struggle feel harder to explain.", "Others may notice withdrawal, slowed speech, low energy, or a person who seems flat or discouraged.", "The label does not mean the problem is minor or imagined. Help can work for this pattern, just as it can for other depressive conditions.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED DISSOCIATIVE DISORDER", "D", "A label for dissociation, such as feeling detached from yourself or time, that fits no standard category.", "Feeling far away from yourself can be frightening, and it can leave gaps in memory. Some people feel numb or as if they are watching from outside.", "Others may notice a blank stare, long pauses, or a person who seems far away mid-conversation.", "Dissociation is a stress response, not something a person chooses or fakes. It often improves with trauma-informed care and steady support.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED MEDICATION-INDUCED MOVEMENT DISORDER", "B", "Movement problems, such as tremor or restlessness, caused by a medicine and not fitting a named category.", "Shaking, stiffness or a constant urge to move can feel distressing and hard to control. Sleep and daily tasks may suffer.", "Others may notice tremors, restless legs or body movements, or stiff posture after a medicine change.", "These movements are caused by a medicine's effect on the nerves, so they are not a personal weakness. Do not stop a prescribed medicine suddenly without advice from the doctor who prescribed it.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED MENTAL DISORDER", "D", "A catch-all label for mental health symptoms that cause distress but fit no named disorder.", "Symptoms can feel distressing even without a standard name. Not having a label can leave people feeling unseen or unsure what help to seek.", "Others may notice persistent distress, changes in mood or behavior, or problems keeping up with work and relationships.", "A label does not make a problem smaller or larger. Help is still available for the symptoms, whatever name they carry.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED MENTAL DISORDER DUE TO ANOTHER MEDICAL CONDITION", "D", "Mental symptoms, such as low mood or confusion, that are caused by a physical illness.", "Mood changes or confusion can feel frightening, especially when they seem to come from nowhere. Relief can come once the physical cause is found and treated.", "Others may notice new confusion, sudden mood shifts, or changes in sleep, appetite or energy.", "A physical cause does not mean the feelings are imaginary. Treating the illness often helps the mood and thinking, and emotional support still matters.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED PERSONALITY DISORDER", "D", "A catch-all label for long-running personality patterns that cause distress but fit no named type.", "Long-running patterns can make work, friendships and love feel hard to manage. Strong reactions may come quickly and then leave a sense of regret.", "Others may notice strong reactions, trouble trusting, or repeated conflict in close relationships.", "The label describes a pattern, not a fixed identity. Personality patterns can change with therapy, practice and time.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED PHOBIA", "P", "A real fear of a specific thing or situation that does not match a named phobia.", "Dread can rise sharply around the feared thing, with a racing heart or an urge to escape. Afterward, the fear may feel out of proportion, yet it was real.", "Others may notice avoidance of certain places or objects, or a sudden freeze or hurried exit.", "A fear with no common name is still real. Feeling silly about it does not make it less important or less treatable.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED PROBLEM RELATED TO PSYCHOSOCIAL CIRCUMSTANCES", "A", "A label for worry and strain caused by life pressures, such as money, housing, work or family.", "Ongoing pressure can bring worry, poor sleep and a constant feeling of being behind. The strain can grow when problems feel out of reach.", "Others may notice missed work or appointments, withdrawal, or changes in sleep and eating.", "Problems caused by life circumstances are not a personal failing. Practical help with money, housing or work can ease the stress that feeds distress.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED SCHIZOPHRENIA SPECTRUM AND OTHER PSYCHOTIC DISORDER", "D", "A catch-all label for psychosis, such as false beliefs or hearing things, that fits no named condition.", "Beliefs or perceptions can feel completely real, even when others see things differently. This can be confusing and frightening for the person and for the people close to them.", "Others may notice unusual beliefs, responses to things others cannot see, or a sharp change in how someone thinks or talks.", "Psychotic symptoms are a medical problem, not a sign of bad character. Treatment can reduce symptoms for many people.", "Call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger. You can also call or text 988 in the US."],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED SOMATIC SYMPTOM", "B", "A label for physical symptoms that cause real distress but do not fit a named group.", "Pain or other body symptoms can feel real and frightening, even when tests show no clear cause. The worry itself can add to the discomfort.", "Others may notice frequent doctor visits, repeated checks of the body, or a person who seems tense about symptoms.", "Symptoms without a clear cause are still real and deserve care. The label does not mean the problem is imagined.", ""],
  ["OTHER SPECIFIED SOMATIC SYMPTOM AND RELATED DISORDER", "B", "A broad label for body symptoms that are closely tied to stress, worry or attention to the body.", "Stress and body sensations can feed each other, so a small twinge may feel big. Tiredness and tension may build without a clear trigger.", "Others may notice frequent body checks, complaints about aches, or a person who seems on guard about their health.", "Stress can cause real physical symptoms, and they are not imagined. Treatment that eases stress and attention can help a great deal.", ""],
  ["THE BURNED OUT", "M", "A state of long exhaustion, cynicism and dread about work or caring for others.", "Energy can feel drained even after sleep, and tasks that once felt meaningful may feel empty. Some people feel numb or resentful, and they long to stop rather than take a short break.", "Others may notice missed deadlines, cynical comments, withdrawal from colleagues, or a flat, tired manner.", "Burnout is not a sign of laziness or weak character. It is a long strain from demands that outpaced rest, and it can improve with real change.", ""],
  ["OVER-APOLOGIZING", "R", "A habit of apologizing again and again, even when you have done nothing wrong.", "Apologies can come from fear of conflict or a need to keep others happy. Each one may bring a brief relief, followed by self-criticism.", "Others may notice apologies for things that were not your fault, or apologies for having needs or opinions.", "A real apology is healthy when you have caused harm. Apologizing for having needs or opinions can wear down your confidence over time.", ""],
  ["OVER-CONTROLLING", "R", "Trying to manage plans, details and other people's choices more tightly than the situation needs.", "Holding tight can bring short relief from anxiety, but loosening control may feel frightening. Others may feel watched or limited, which strains trust.", "Others may notice constant checking on plans, frustration when things change, or insistence that tasks be done one way.", "Wanting things to go well is not the same as over-controlling. The need often comes from fear, and it can ease with support and practice.", ""],
  ["OVERFLOWING SELF-APPRECIATION", "R", "Praise for oneself that is so constant and large that it crowds out room for other people's views.", "Self-admiration can feel bright and certain, and it may bring a sense of pride. Underneath, it can hide a fear of being ordinary.", "Others may notice frequent self-praise, talk that centers on one's own achievements, and little interest in others' stories.", "Healthy self-respect is valuable. The pattern becomes harmful when others' worth is ignored or diminished to keep the self in focus.", ""],
  ["OVERSTIMULATED", "M", "Feeling overloaded by noise, light, people or information, so the mind and body cannot settle.", "Too much input can feel buzzing, frayed or trapped. Small irritations may suddenly feel huge, and rest may feel out of reach.", "Others may notice covering the ears, pacing, snapping at people, or going quiet and withdrawn.", "Feeling overstimulated is not a character flaw. Some nervous systems feel it more often, and it can be eased with planned quiet time.", ""],
  ["OVERTHINKING", "T", "Replaying the same thoughts again and again, without reaching a useful answer or a decision.", "The mind can feel crowded, restless and tired. Each loop may bring a new worry, which keeps the thinking going.", "Others may notice distraction, delayed decisions, or a person who seems lost in thought and tired after talking.", "Thinking carefully about a problem is useful. Overthinking is different: it circles without moving forward, and it can be interrupted.", ""],
  ["PALLOR (PALENESS)", "B", "An unusually pale or washed-out look of the skin, often linked to illness, blood loss or shock.", "The person may feel cold, dizzy or weak when skin looks very pale. Some people do not notice the change until someone points it out.", "Others may notice a gray or washed-out tone in the face, lips or nail beds.", "Paleness can have many causes, so it should not be dismissed as tiredness. Sudden paleness with fainting, chest pain or confusion needs urgent medical care.", ""],
  ["PARALYZED", "A", "Feeling unable to move or act, often frozen by fear or overwhelm.", "The mind may be awake while the body feels heavy, stuck or slow to respond. This can bring panic or shame about not reacting.", "Others may notice freezing, going silent, or a pause before answering that seems much longer than usual.", "Freezing is an automatic stress reaction, not a choice or a weakness. Sudden weakness or loss of movement, though, is a medical emergency.", ""],
  ["PARANOIA", "T", "A strong, lasting belief that others are plotting against you, often without solid evidence.", "Suspicion can feel certain and urgent, and it can be hard to test the belief against facts. Trust in others may feel impossible, which can be lonely.", "Others may notice guardedness, questions about motives, sudden accusations, or checking for hidden threats.", "Caution is sometimes useful, but suspicion that ignores evidence can be a symptom of illness. Psychosis, which means losing touch with what is real, is one cause, and treatment can help.", "Call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger. You can also call or text 988 in the US."],
  ["PARANOIAC", "R", "A person who often believes others are plotting against them, even without clear evidence.", "Constant wariness can feel exhausting, and the person may feel unsafe even in friendly places. Close relationships can become strained.", "Others may notice frequent accusations, hostility toward helpers, or a belief that friends secretly oppose them.", "Being on guard is not the same as being a bad person. Paranoid thinking is often treatable, and support can help the person and the people around them.", ""],
  ["PARANOID", "T", "Expecting that others are against you or secretly working to harm you, with little proof.", "A paranoid mind can feel alert and unsafe at once. Small signals, such as a glance or a delay, may feel like proof of a plot.", "Others may notice a person reading hidden meanings into neutral comments, or refusing to trust friendly offers.", "Feeling watched or doubted can happen to anyone under stress. The pattern matters when suspicion stays strong despite facts, and it is worth raising with a doctor.", ""],
  ["PARANOID PERSONALITY DISORDER", "D", "A long-running pattern of deep distrust of others that makes close bonds hard to build.", "Trusting people can feel unsafe, so the person may stay alert and guarded. Loneliness can grow, even when the person wants closeness.", "Others may notice suspicion of friends' motives, reluctance to share personal details, or quick anger at perceived slights.", "Distrust is not always wrong, but this pattern pushes people away. Talk therapy can help many people build trust slowly over time.", ""],
  ["PARENTIFICATION", "R", "A child takes on adult roles, such as caring for siblings or comforting a parent emotionally.", "The child may feel responsible and proud, yet also tired, anxious or lonely. Needs of their own can feel hard to name or to express.", "Others may notice a young person handling bills, caring for younger children, or comforting a parent in adult ways.", "Helping at home is not harmful in itself. The pattern becomes a problem when a child carries adult duties and emotional burdens for too long.", ""],
  ["PARIS SYNDROME", "A", "A sudden shock or distress when a visit to Paris falls far short of a long-held dream.", "Expectations and reality clash, which can bring panic, dizziness or tears. The feeling can be intense but usually fades with rest and support.", "Others may notice sudden distress at a famous sight, tears, confusion, or a wish to leave at once.", "It is not a sign of fragility or a lack of taste. Strong expectations can make a real place feel shocking, and this can happen to anyone.", ""],
  ["PARKINSON'S DISEASE DEMENTIA", "B", "Memory and thinking decline that can develop in people who already have Parkinson's disease.", "Confusion, slowed thinking and forgetfulness can be frightening for the person and worrying for family. Changes in mood or sleep may come too.", "Others may notice memory lapses, slower speech or thought, hallucinations (seeing or hearing things that are not there), or unsteady movement.", "This is a disease complication, not a normal part of aging. Medical care and daily support can ease symptoms and help the whole family.", ""],
  ["PARTS INTEGRATION", "K", "A practice of helping conflicting inner parts of yourself understand and work together.", "Parts that felt at war may begin to listen to each other, which can bring calm and clarity. Naming each part can make a confusing mix of feelings easier to hold.", "Others may notice a person speaking kindly about different sides of themselves, or describing inner voices calmly.", "It helps understand mixed feelings. It is not the same as being split into separate people.", ""],
  ["PASSIONATE", "U", "Feeling intense enthusiasm or love for something meaningful, and acting on it with energy.", "Passion can feel alive and energizing, and it can make ordinary days feel full. It can also bring restlessness when the passion is blocked.", "Others may notice enthusiasm in conversation, long hours on a project, or eyes that light up when speaking about something loved.", "Passion is not the same as recklessness. It can be balanced with rest, and it grows when it is shared with others.", ""],
  ["PASSIVE", "R", "A habit of letting others decide and not speaking up for your own wants or needs.", "Going along can feel safer than risking conflict. Resentment may build underneath, even when the outside looks calm.", "Others may notice a person who says 'I don't mind' often, waits for others to choose, or agrees without enthusiasm.", "Being passive is not the same as being kind or easygoing. The pattern can be changed with small steps, such as stating one preference each day.", ""],
  ["PASSIVE-AGGRESSIVE", "R", "Showing anger or resistance indirectly, through sulking, sarcasm, delay or quiet sabotage.", "The person may feel angry but unable to say so directly, which builds tension. The other person may feel confused about what went wrong.", "Others may notice sarcastic remarks, the silent treatment, or a task done badly on purpose.", "Not every quiet response is passive-aggressive. The pattern is hidden anger that makes honest talk harder.", ""],
  ["PASSIVE AGGRESSIVE PERSONALITY DISORDER", "D", "An older label for a long-running pattern of indirect resistance and hidden anger toward others.", "Anger may feel hard to voice, so it leaks out in quiet ways. Relationships can feel tense, and the person may feel misunderstood.", "Others may notice a pattern of missed promises, backhanded comments, and resentment that surfaces later.", "It is an older label, and clinicians now usually describe the specific behavior instead. Therapy can help people speak their needs directly.", ""],
  ["PATHOLOGICAL DEMAND AVOIDANCE", "A", "An anxiety-driven pattern of strongly resisting everyday requests, even ones the person wants to do.", "Requests can feel threatening, so the urge to resist can be strong, even against one's own goals. Guilt and exhaustion often follow.", "Others may notice stalling, distraction, sudden changes of subject, or social strategies to avoid a request.", "This is not simple defiance or bad behavior. It is driven by anxiety and is used as a profile, not a formal diagnosis in every system.", ""],
  ["PATHOLOGICAL LIAR", "R", "A person who lies often and easily, even when the truth would cost them little.", "The person may feel anxious about being caught, and may feel a story is needed for safety. Trust in them can wear down over time.", "Others may notice stories that do not match facts, quick defensiveness when questioned, and frequent contradictions.", "Lying is not always about malice. Frequent lying can come from fear, shame or a memory problem, and some causes need a medical check.", ""],
  ["PATIENCE", "U", "Staying calm and steady while waiting, or while things move more slowly than you would like.", "Patience can bring a quiet steadiness and room to think. It can feel hard when delays seem unfair or endless.", "Others may notice a calm voice during delays, a willingness to wait for a turn, or steady effort over time.", "Patience is not passivity or silent suffering. It is a choice to stay engaged while waiting, and it can be practiced.", ""],
  ["PATIENT", "U", "Able to wait and tolerate delays, setbacks or other people's slower pace without losing composure.", "Patience can feel warm and forgiving toward others and toward yourself. It may bring relief when pressure eases.", "Others may notice a person who listens fully, waits without sighing, and does not rush people through tasks.", "Being patient does not mean putting up with harm. You can be patient with a process and still set limits when someone mistreats you.", ""],
  ["PATIENT ENDURANCE", "U", "Steady perseverance through long, hard stretches, without giving up on what matters most.", "Endurance can feel tiring and sometimes lonely. It can also bring a deep sense of quiet strength once a hard stretch passes.", "Others may notice a person keeping routines through a difficult season, returning to work or care, and staying kind.", "Endurance is not the same as enduring abuse or staying in harm. It means persisting with goals and care while resting and asking for help.", ""],
  ["PEACEFUL", "M", "A calm, quiet state in which the mind and body feel settled and free from strain.", "Peace can feel soft and unhurried. Thoughts may still arrive, but they pass without pulling you far.", "Others may notice relaxed shoulders, a steady voice, and an easy pace in conversation.", "Peace is not the same as having no problems or avoiding feelings. It is a steadier place to meet problems from.", ""],
  ["PEACEFUL CENTERED CALM", "U", "A calm that stays anchored in your center, even when people or events around you are busy.", "Centered calm can feel grounded, as if you have a solid place to stand. Noise and demands may still come, but they can be met without panic.", "Others may notice a person who stays composed in a crowded room and responds slowly, without rushing.", "Centered calm is not coldness or indifference. It is a steady presence that still cares and still acts when action is needed.", ""],
  ["PEACEFUL DETACHMENT", "U", "A calm distance from strong pulls, such as craving or anger, while staying connected to people.", "Feelings can be noticed without being swept away, which can bring relief. The mind may feel spacious and less reactive.", "Others may notice a person who does not rush to react, stays composed during conflict, and listens before answering.", "This is not numbness, dissociation or emotional shutdown. It is a calm distance that still feels connected to others.", ""],
  ["PEACEFUL INNER HARMONY", "U", "An inner sense that your thoughts, feelings and values are working together rather than pulling apart.", "Harmony can feel smooth and whole, with less inner tug-of-war. Decisions may feel clearer when the inner voices agree.", "Others may notice a person who speaks with consistency, keeps promises and acts in line with what they say.", "Inner harmony does not mean having no conflict at all. It means conflicts are faced with care rather than fought in secret.", ""],
  ["PEACEFUL SELF-TRUST", "U", "A calm confidence that you can handle what comes, based on your own steady experience.", "Self-trust can feel quiet and settled, without a need to prove anything. It can ease the urge to second-guess every choice.", "Others may notice a person who makes decisions without constant reassurance and admits mistakes calmly.", "Self-trust is not certainty or arrogance. It means knowing you can learn and recover, even when you are unsure.", ""],
  ["PEACEMAKER", "U", "A person who works to calm conflict, bridge differences and help people reach common ground.", "Peacemaking can feel meaningful and steadying, though it can also be draining. Holding a room together takes energy.", "Others may notice a person who listens to all sides, lowers the temperature in tense talks, and finds middle ground.", "Peacemaking is not keeping quiet about harm or smoothing over abuse. It means helping people be heard, with fairness and honesty.", ""],
  ["PEDIOPHOBE", "P", "A strong fear of dolls or doll-like figures, including lifelike toys and statues.", "A doll or lifelike figure can bring unease, a racing heart or an urge to look away. The feeling may seem odd, but it is real.", "Others may notice avoidance of toy shops or displays, turning away from dolls, or moving quickly past lifelike statues.", "This is a fear of dolls and lifelike figures, not a fear of children. The fear is real, and gradual, supported exposure often helps.", ""],
  ["PEDOPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in children before puberty, which is always serious and harmful when acted on.", "This interest can bring shame, dread and a sense of isolation. Many people who notice it feel alone, and early, confidential help can prevent harm.", "Often nothing can be seen from outside, since the interest is usually kept private. Any pattern of secret or unsupervised contact with children is a serious warning sign.", "Interest is not the same as acting on it, and help can start before anyone is harmed. Acting on sexual interest in children always harms them and is a serious crime.", "Sexual interest in children is serious, and acting on it harms children. Stop It Now offers a free, confidential helpline for people worried about their own thoughts or behavior, or someone else's: 1-888-773-8368. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["PEELING SKIN SYNDROME", "B", "A rare skin disorder in which the outer layer of skin peels or sheds, sometimes over large areas.", "Peeling can be itchy, sore or embarrassing, and it can make a person self-conscious in public. Daily tasks may feel harder when the skin is raw.", "Others may notice flaking or peeling skin, redness, or raw patches on the hands, feet or body.", "It usually has a medical cause, not poor hygiene or a bad habit. A skin doctor, called a dermatologist, can help reduce the peeling and discomfort.", ""],
  ["PENDULATION SKILL", "K", "A body-based practice of moving attention gently between a calm feeling and a tense one.", "Shifting between comfort and tension can help the body feel safer, one small step at a time. Many people notice a gentle softening as they rest in the calm spot.", "Others may notice a person slowly looking around, resting attention on a calm spot, or taking a slow breath before continuing.", "It does not mean diving into the worst feeling. Pendulation keeps hard feelings in small, safe doses, with rest in between.", ""],
  ["PEOPLE-PLEASING", "R", "A habit of saying yes and shaping yourself to keep others happy, even at your own cost.", "Saying no can bring a jolt of guilt or fear of disapproval. Being liked can feel like safety, even when it leaves you worn out and resentful.", "Agreeing to things you do not want, apologizing for having needs, and changing your opinion to match the room.", "Kindness and generosity are not people-pleasing. It becomes a problem when you cannot say no without deep distress, or lose track of what you want.", ""],
  ["PERFECTIONISM", "T", "A belief that anything less than flawless is unacceptable, and a drive to reach that standard.", "Mistakes can feel like personal failures. Starting a task may feel heavy, and finishing it may bring relief that fades quickly.", "Rewriting emails many times, delaying projects until they are perfect, and harsh self-talk after small errors.", "High standards are not the same as perfectionism. The pattern is harmful when fear of imperfection blocks progress or drains your health.", ""],
  ["PERFECTIONISM AS DEFENSE", "T", "Striving for flawlessness as a shield against criticism, shame or rejection.", "Being perfect can feel like the only way to stay safe from blame. Underneath, there may be a constant worry that people will see the real you and pull away.", "Over-preparing to avoid judgment, hiding work until it is flawless, and becoming defensive when feedback arrives.", "Wanting to do well is healthy. It becomes a defense when the effort is mainly about avoiding shame, which leaves little room for trying, learning or being seen.", ""],
  ["PERFECTIONIST", "R", "A person who holds themselves, and often others, to flawless standards.", "Being around a perfectionist can feel tense, as if every detail is being checked. Praise may feel brief, while small mistakes seem to take over the mood.", "Frequent corrections, unrealistic deadlines, and visible frustration when work or plans fall short of an exact ideal.", "A person who cares about quality is not automatically a perfectionist. It is harmful when standards become constant pressure on themselves or on the people around them.", ""],
  ["PERFORMATIVE EMPATHY", "R", "Showing concern and emotion mainly to look caring, rather than to understand or help.", "Their words may sound warm, but you can leave the conversation feeling unseen. Attention often shifts quickly back to them.", "Polished sympathy with no follow-up, dramatic displays of feeling in public, and no change when you ask for practical help.", "Expressing feelings is not fake by default. The pattern is harmful when care is used to look good or to avoid doing what was actually needed.", ""],
  ["PERSECUTED", "T", "Feeling that others are against you or plotting harm, even without clear evidence.", "A steady sense of threat can make the world feel unsafe. Ordinary comments can seem like attacks, and it can be lonely to feel targeted.", "Reading hostile intent into neutral remarks, expecting unfair treatment, and gathering evidence that others are out to get you.", "Sometimes people really are treated unfairly, and that deserves to be taken seriously. It is a thinking pattern when the sense of threat is constant and does not match the facts.", ""],
  ["PERSEVERATION", "T", "Getting stuck repeating the same thought, word or action after the reason for it has passed.", "The mind keeps returning to the same loop, like a record that will not move on. It can feel tiring and hard to switch off.", "Repeating the same question or story, returning to one worry again and again, or doing one action over and over.", "Revisiting a problem to solve it is normal. Perseveration is different, because the loop continues without helping, even after the question has been answered.", ""],
  ["PERSEVERATOR", "R", "A person who keeps returning to the same point, topic or demand long after it has been addressed.", "Conversations with them can feel endless. You may feel pressed to keep answering, and it becomes hard to end a discussion.", "Repeating the same complaint or request after answers have been given, reopening settled topics, and continuing even when asked to stop.", "Being persistent about something important is not the same as perseverating. It is a problem when repetition replaces listening and leaves others worn down.", ""],
  ["PERSISTENT DEPRESSIVE DISORDER", "D", "A low mood that lasts for years, often with low energy, poor appetite or sleep changes.", "Life can feel flat and heavy for long stretches. Many people assume this is just who they are, which can make hope harder to reach.", "A sad or empty mood on most days for a long time, low energy, changes in sleep or appetite, and little enjoyment of things.", "Feeling low for a while is common, and it is not a personal failing. Persistent depressive disorder is a treatable condition that often needs ongoing care, not willpower alone.", ""],
  ["PHARMACOMANIAC", "F", "A strong pull toward seeking out or taking medicines, often more than they are meant for.", "The urge to take a pill can feel urgent and comforting. Between doses, the mind may keep returning to the idea of getting more.", "Asking for extra prescriptions, taking medicines in ways not prescribed, or feeling anxious whenever a supply runs low.", "Needing medicine as prescribed is not pharmacomania. The pull becomes harmful when it leads to taking more than advised, or to getting medicine from others or from unsafe sources.", "If you have taken too much of a medicine or feel unsafe, call 911 now. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["PHILOCALIST", "F", "A strong pull toward beauty in art, nature, design or everyday surroundings.", "Beautiful things can bring a rush of wonder and calm. The pull may be strong enough that ordinary surroundings start to feel dull.", "Spending time seeking out art or scenery, collecting beautiful objects, and rearranging a home to feel more lovely.", "Loving beauty is a gift and not a problem. It becomes a concern only if it drives heavy spending, debt, or neglect of daily needs.", ""],
  ["PHILOPHOBE", "P", "A fear of love, of falling in love, or of getting close to someone.", "Closeness can bring a sense of dread, as if being loved will lead to loss or pain. The body may tense when affection is offered.", "Pulling away after a good moment, keeping partners at arm's length, and avoiding dates or more vulnerable talks.", "Caution in relationships is not always a phobia. It is worth exploring when the fear of love keeps you lonely or repeatedly ends connections that you want.", ""],
  ["PHOBOPHILE", "F", "A person drawn to the things they fear, and to seeking out frightening experiences.", "Fear can feel both scary and exciting at once. The pull toward frightening situations may return often, even after they have been upsetting.", "Seeking out scary films, risky thrills or unsafe situations, and returning to them even when they cause real distress.", "Enjoying a safe scare, such as a horror film, is common. It becomes harmful when the seeking puts you in real danger or leaves you more anxious and unable to rest.", ""],
  ["PHONOMANIAC", "F", "An intense pull toward sounds, such as loud music, noise or the sound of voices.", "Certain sounds can feel irresistible, drawing your full attention. The urge to listen or make noise may build until it is satisfied.", "Seeking out loud music or noise for long periods, replaying favorite sounds, or making noises for their own sake.", "Loving music and sound is common and enriching. It becomes a concern when it harms your hearing or sleep, affects other people, or fills most of your day.", ""],
  ["PHYSIOLOGICAL SIGH", "K", "A breathing practice of two inhales through the nose, then one long, slow exhale.", "A double inhale can ease tightness in the chest quickly. The long exhale often brings a noticeable sense of release and calm.", "A quick deep breath in, a second short top-up breath, then a slow, relaxed breath out through the mouth.", "A single sigh does not fix anxiety or other conditions. It is a helpful tool for short moments of stress, and it works best when practiced regularly.", ""],
  ["PICA", "D", "An eating disorder in which a person repeatedly eats things that are not food.", "The urge to chew or swallow items such as ice, soil or paper can feel strong and hard to resist. Afterward, there may be shame or worry about harm.", "Repeatedly eating non-food items such as dirt, chalk, hair or paint, often over months, sometimes hidden from others.", "Eating an unusual thing once is not pica. It is a real condition that can cause poisoning, blockages or infections, and it needs medical care.", "Eating non-food items can cause serious harm, so see a doctor soon. In an emergency, call 911. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["PITY", "M", "A feeling of sorrow for someone's hardship, often mixed with a wish to help.", "Pity can feel tender, but it can also carry a sense of distance or looking down on someone. Many people feel uneasy when pity turns into a need to fix things.", "Saying 'poor thing' when someone shares a problem, offering help out of sympathy alone, or feeling sorry without asking what they need.", "Pity is not the same as compassion. Compassion respects the other person's strength, while pity can slip into condescension or make someone feel smaller.", ""],
  ["PITY PLOY", "C", "Using a show of hardship or sadness to get others to give in, excuse them, or keep the peace.", "For the receiver, it can feel like a sudden, heavy duty to rescue. You may give in to end the distress, then feel used and resentful later.", "Dramatic stories of suffering when a limit is set, repeated lines such as 'after all I have done for you', and sudden collapse whenever a decision goes against them.", "Real hardship deserves care, and asking for help is not a ploy. It is a pattern when pain is used to avoid responsibility or to override your choices.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["PLAYFUL", "U", "A light, joyful way of approaching life, with room for fun, curiosity and surprise.", "Playfulness can feel loose and easy, as if the world has room to breathe. It may bring laughter, or a sense that small things are worth enjoying.", "Trying a silly idea, teasing gently with a friend, or turning a dull chore into a game.", "Being playful does not mean avoiding serious matters or joking through pain. It works best alongside care, honesty and attention to the people around you.", ""],
  ["PLAYFUL CREATIVITY", "U", "A willingness to explore ideas freely through play, experiment and making things.", "Creative play can feel open and absorbing, as if time slips by. Fear of doing it wrong may fade for a while.", "Doodling without a plan, trying a new recipe or color, and making something just to see what happens.", "Creativity does not need talent or a finished product to count. It is a practice of exploring, and it often grows from trying small, imperfect things.", ""],
  ["PLAYFUL CURIOSITY", "U", "A light, open interest in learning, asking questions and noticing new things.", "Curiosity can feel like a warm pull toward something new. It makes ordinary days feel more like an adventure.", "Asking 'what if' questions, following a random interest down a rabbit hole, and trying something unfamiliar with an open mind.", "Curiosity is not the same as nosiness or gathering information to control someone. It is healthiest when it leads to learning and respect for others' privacy.", ""],
  ["PLAYFUL FREEDOM", "U", "A sense of being free to play, try and express yourself without rigid expectations.", "Freedom in play feels light and unhurried. There is less pressure to perform and more room to be yourself.", "Choosing an activity just for fun, laughing at yourself, and changing plans when something more enjoyable appears.", "Freedom in play is not the same as ignoring responsibilities or hurting others. It works best when it fits your commitments and respects other people's limits.", ""],
  ["PLAYFUL INNER LIGHTNESS", "U", "A gentle, lighter inner tone that lets you take yourself less seriously.", "Inner lightness can feel like a softer voice in your head. Mistakes sting less, and you may smile at your own quirks.", "Laughing at small slip-ups, speaking kindly to yourself, and bouncing back quickly after a hard moment.", "Lightness is not the same as avoiding hard feelings or brushing off real problems. It grows from kindness toward yourself, not from pretending everything is fine.", ""],
  ["PLAYFUL LIGHTNESS", "U", "An easy, buoyant way of carrying daily life, with humor and a relaxed pace.", "Lightness can feel like taking a deep breath after a heavy week. Problems may seem more manageable when approached with a little humor.", "Light teasing that makes people laugh together, a relaxed shrug at delays, and a steady, unhurried voice in tense moments.", "Lightness is not making light of someone's pain or dodging hard talks. It works best when humor brings people closer rather than leaving someone out.", ""],
  ["PLAYFUL SOUL FREEDOM", "U", "A sense of freedom in play that comes from being at ease with who you are.", "Feeling free in play can bring a quiet, deep contentment. You may feel less need to meet others' expectations and more willingness to be playful.", "Dancing or singing when no one is watching, sharing a silly side with people you trust, and choosing hobbies that feel personally meaningful.", "Being free is not the same as being careless with yourself or others. It grows from self-acceptance, and it stays healthy when it fits your values and relationships.", ""],
  ["PLAYFULNESS REAWAKENING", "K", "A practice of gently reconnecting with play after stress, routine or low mood has dulled it.", "Reawakening play can feel awkward at first, like stretching a stiff muscle. Small moments of fun may return slowly and feel surprisingly good.", "Returning to a childhood hobby, trying a simple game again, or joining a light activity after a long stretch of seriousness.", "Playfulness cannot be forced on demand, and low mood is not a failure of fun. If joy stays out of reach for weeks, it is worth talking with a doctor or counselor.", ""],
  ["PLAYING THE VICTIM", "R", "A pattern of presenting yourself as wronged or helpless to avoid responsibility or gain sympathy.", "Being around someone who plays the victim can feel exhausting. You may feel blamed for their troubles, and honest discussion of problems becomes hard.", "Turning every concern into a story about being attacked, refusing any share of responsibility, and asking others to fix what went wrong.", "Feeling hurt is real, and it is not the same as playing the victim. The pattern is harmful when hurt is used to avoid accountability or to control others.", ""],
  ["PLEASURE REAWAKENING", "K", "A practice of rediscovering everyday enjoyment after numbness or low mood has dulled it.", "Pleasure may feel distant or muted at first. Small enjoyments can slowly return, often with a sense of relief.", "Noticing a favorite food, scent or view again, returning to a hobby, or letting yourself enjoy a simple moment without rushing on.", "Pleasure is not something you must feel on command, and numbness can be a sign of depression or stress. A lasting loss of enjoyment is worth discussing with a doctor or counselor.", ""],
  ["PLIGHT RESPONSE", "A", "A stress state in which a person feels trapped, helpless and unable to change their situation.", "Feeling trapped can bring heavy hopelessness, and the body may go numb or still. Thoughts can narrow to one painful picture of having no way out.", "Going quiet or freezing in a hard moment, saying 'there is no point', pulling away from help, or seeming flat and unreachable.", "Feeling stuck is common under pressure, and it does not mean the situation is permanent. It needs support when it lasts, or when it brings thoughts of harm to yourself.", "If you have thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["PLUTOMANIAC", "F", "A strong pull toward wealth, money and possessions, and the status they bring.", "The desire for money can feel urgent and bright, promising security and admiration. Between goals, it may leave you restless or comparing yourself to others.", "Constantly checking balances or prices, chasing every new chance for gain, and talking mostly about money or status.", "Wanting financial security is reasonable. It becomes harmful when money crowds out health, rest or relationships, or when it pushes you into risky debt.", ""],
  ["PLUVIOPHILE", "F", "A deep love of rain, storms and wet, grey weather.", "Rainy days can feel soothing and reflective, bringing a sense of calm. Some people feel most like themselves when the sky is grey and wet.", "Choosing to sit near a window during storms, going out in the rain on purpose, or seeking out rainy places to travel.", "Liking rain is a gentle, common preference. It becomes a concern only if the pull toward bad weather leads to unsafe choices, or if low mood follows grey days for weeks.", ""],
  ["POLYVAGAL SAFETY", "A", "A calm, safe feeling that comes when the nervous system feels settled enough to rest and connect.", "Safety can feel warm and open, with easy breathing and a willingness to connect. The body feels less on guard.", "A relaxed face and voice, easy eye contact, a soft tone, and a body that can rest without visible tension.", "Polyvagal theory is a model that some scientists question, so its neat stages should not be taken as fact. The feeling of safety itself is real and can be built through rest, routine and supportive relationships.", ""],
  ["POROUS BOUNDARIES", "R", "A pattern of letting others' needs, moods or demands easily cross into your own space and choices.", "Having porous boundaries can leave you drained and responsible for everyone's feelings. Saying no can feel almost impossible, and it is hard to tell where you end and others begin.", "Saying yes when you mean no, sharing private details too quickly, and taking on other people's problems as if they were your own.", "Being generous and flexible is healthy. Boundaries become a problem when they are so loose that your time, safety or wellbeing is regularly overridden.", ""],
  ["POSSESSED", "M", "A feeling of being taken over by an intense emotion, thought or urge that seems to control you.", "It can feel as if something stronger than you has taken over your thoughts and body. Control seems to slip away, leaving fear or shame afterward.", "Sudden intense outbursts, a fixed focus on one feeling or person, and saying 'I could not help myself' after the moment has passed.", "Strong feelings are normal and do not mean you have lost yourself. When this feeling is frequent or frightening, it deserves a conversation with a counselor or doctor.", ""],
  ["POSSESSIVE", "R", "A person who wants to own or tightly control another person's time, attention or choices.", "Being around a possessive person can feel smothering. You may feel watched, questioned about every move, and afraid of upsetting them.", "Constant questions about where you were, anger at time spent with friends, checking your phone, and pressure to cut off other people.", "Wanting closeness and reassurance is normal in relationships. It becomes harmful when it limits your freedom, isolates you from others or leads to threats.", "If this is happening to you, a counselor or domestic violence advocate can help you plan. The National Domestic Violence Hotline is 1-800-799-7233 in the US. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["POSSESSIVENESS", "M", "An intense feeling of ownership over a person, relationship or thing, often driven by fear of loss.", "The urge to hold on tightly can feel urgent and painful. Fear that something will be taken away can crowd out calm and trust.", "Feeling jealous when a loved one spends time elsewhere, wanting to know every detail, and worrying that others will take what is yours.", "Caring deeply about someone is not the same as possessiveness. The feeling becomes harmful when it pushes you to control, monitor or punish the other person.", ""],
  ["SEPARATION ANXIETY DISORDER", "D", "An anxiety disorder marked by intense fear of being apart from people one feels close to.", "Time apart can bring a strong sense of dread, with racing thoughts about what might go wrong. The fear can be hard to explain and may feel out of proportion.", "Repeated distress when a loved one leaves, reluctance to go to school or work, frequent check-ins, and nightmares about separation.", "Missing someone or feeling sad at goodbyes is normal. It becomes a disorder when the fear is intense, lasts for months and interferes with daily life. Many people improve with treatment.", ""],
  ["SEXUAL MASOCHISM", "F", "A sexual interest in receiving pain or humiliation as part of intimate experiences.", "The interest can feel intense, confusing or shameful, especially if it was never talked about openly. Some people feel relief once they understand it better.", "A private, persistent interest in being hurt or humiliated during intimacy, along with worry or secrecy about it.", "Having an interest is not the same as acting on it. Any activity must be fully consensual, safe and clearly agreed, and acting without consent is harm.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
];
